WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.490
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:09.719
We have had plenty of episodes about fluid
simulations, so how about some tasty soft

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:11.749
body dynamics for today?

00:00:11.749 --> 00:00:17.130
Soft body dynamics basically means computing
what happens when we smash together different

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.330
deformable objects.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:24.439
Examples include folding sheets, playing around
with noodles, or torturing armadillos.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:30.570
I think this is a nice and representative
showcase of the immense joys of computer graphics

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:31.570
research!

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:35.539
The key to real-time physically based simulations
is parallelism.

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:41.020
Parallelism means that we have many of the
same units working together in harmony.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:47.239
Imagine if we had to assign 50 people to work
together to make a coffee in the same kitchen.

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:52.480
As you may imagine, they would trip over each
other, and the result would be chaos, not

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:53.590
productivity.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:58.559
Such a process would not scale favorably,
because as we would add more people after

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:04.140
around 3 or 4, the productivity would not
increase, but drop significantly.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:09.420
You can often hear a similar example of 9
pregnant women not being able to give birth

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.320
to a baby in one month.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.930
For better scaling, we have to subdivide a
bigger task into small tasks in a way that

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.590
these people can work independently.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:24.100
The more independently they can work, the
better the productivity will scale as we add

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.140
more people.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:30.740
In software engineering, these virtual people
we like call threads, or compute units.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:38.230
As of 2016, mid-tier processors are equipped
with 4-8 logical cores, and for a video card,

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:42.060
we typically have compute units in the order
of hundreds.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.040
So if we wish to develop efficient algorithms,
we have to make sure that these big simulation

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.600
tasks are subdivided in a way so that these
threads are not tripping over each other.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.250
And the big contribution of this piece of
work is a technique to distribute the computation

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:02.760
tasks to these compute units in a way that
they are working on independent chunks of

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:03.760
the problem.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.929
This is achieved via using graph coloring,
which is a technique typically used for designing

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:15.709
seating plans, exam timetabling, solving sudoku
puzzles and similar assignment tasks.

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:21.530
It not only works in an absolutely spectacular
manner, but graph theory is an immensely beautiful

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:25.900
subfield of mathematics, so additional style
points to the authors!

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:31.430
The technique produces remarkably realistic
animations and requires only 15 milliseconds

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:37.290
per frame, which means that this technique
can render over 60 frames per second comfortably.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.739
And the other most important factor is that
this technique is also stable, meaning that

00:02:41.739 --> 00:02:46.909
it offers an appropriate solution, even when
many other techniques fail to deliver.

00:02:46.909 --> 00:02:50.749
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

